      "text": "We know that $i^0 = 1, i^1 = i, i^2 = -1, i^3 = -i, i^4 = 1, i^5 = i, i^6 = -1, i^7 = -i, i^8 = 1,$ and so on.\n\nFirst, let's consider the case when $x$ is odd. In this case, $i^x$ will be a real number, so $i^x$ will have opposite sign with $i^y$ for $i^x$ and $i^y$ to add up real numbers. Specifically, if $i^x$ has positive value, then to balance the sum, $i^y$ must be negative, and vice versa.\n\nThus, for each odd $x$, there is a corresponding even $y$ that makes $i^x + i^y$ a real number. This pattern continues for odd values of $x$ and even values of $y$, so we can pair them up together to find the solution.\n\nNow, let's do the same for the case when $x$ is even. In this case, $i^x$ will have the opposite sign as $i^y$, so $i^x + i^y$ will be a real number. This means that every $x$ value contributes to one real number, and because $1 \\leq x < y \\leq 100$, we have half of the real numbers for the odd and even case when we consider $1 \\leq x \\leq 100$ ignoring the overcounted case.\n\nTherefore, we have total 50 real numbers resulting from odd $i^x$ by $i^y$ and 50 resulting from even $i^x$ by $i^y$. So, the number of ordered pairs of integers is 50.\n\nTime taken: 1 minute\n\n$\\boxed{50}$",
