      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "We need to find the values of $(i^x+i^y)$ that are real, i.e., 1, 0, or -1.\n\nFor X to be real:\n\n$i^x$\n$x / 4k \\equiv 0 \\pmod{4}$\n\nFor Y to be real:\n\n$i^y$\n$y / 4k \\equiv 0 \\pmod{4}$\n\nValue of $k$ is between $0$ and $4$.\n\nEach $x$ and $y$ pair can contribute a real value to $i^x+i^y$ if and only if $k = 1$.  If our mathignieur were more alert, he would have \"regretted\" three chances ,subbing values of $1,0,-1$.\n\n$11^2 = 121$ pairs.\n\nBut these are $(x,x)(x,y)$ and $(x,y, y)(y,y)$.  There are degeneracies.  Fix pairs $(x,x),(y,y)$.\nThe triplet on the left sets\n\n$xx\\succ 0$ and $yy\\succ 0$ \n$xy=\\pm 1$\n\nThere are $100$ ways to pick $x$ and $y$ (no compaction), and the distinct $z$ that make this a valid solution is left.\n\nWe fixed x and y, so there are $2$ such integers, and the other $80$ can be picked in $\\binom{80}{2}$ ways, and $100$ other ways to pick $z$.\n\nThere are $\\binom{100}{2}+80 \\cdot \\binom{80}{2}+100$ solutions, all regular.",
