      "text": "To find the number of ordered pairs \\((x, y)\\) where \\(1 \\le x < y \\le 100\\) such that \\(i^x + i^y\\) is a real number, we need to analyze the powers of the imaginary unit \\(i\\). The powers of \\(i\\) cycle every four terms: \\(i^1 = i\\), \\(i^2 = -1\\), \\(i^3 = -i\\), and \\(i^4 = 1\\). Therefore, \\(i^x + i^y\\) will be real if one of the two terms is \\(i\\) and the other is \\(-i\\), i.e., \\(i^x + i^y = i - i^y = 2i(1 + i)\\) is real, or if \\(i^x + i^y = i - i^x = 2i(i - 1)\\) is real, or if \\(i^x + i^y = 1 + i^y = 0\\) is real. This implies that one of \\(x\\) or \\(y\\) must be even.\n\nFor any integer \\(z\\) in the range \\([1, 100]\\), the pairs \\((x, y)\\) that \\(1 \\le x < y \\le 100\\) and satisfy \\(i^x + i^y = 0\\) are \\( (1, 3), (1, 5), \\ldots, (97, 99), (3, 5), (3, 7), \\ldots, (97, 99), \\ldots, (101, 103), \\ldots \\). The number of such pairs is equal to \\(16\\) (since for every odd number \\(x\\), there is a pair \\((x, 101-x)\\) where \\(101-x\\) is even).\n\nSince \\(y\\) can be chosen independently of \\(x\\) with the same probability from each of the four subgroups of odd numbers, and there are 50 odd and 50 even numbers from \\(1\\) to \\(50\\), the number of valid pairs \\((x, y)\\) is \\(25 \\times 50 = 800\\).\n\nThus, the number of ordered pairs \\((x, y)\\) such that \\(1 \\le x < y \\le 100\\) and \\(i^x + i^y\\) is a real number is \\(800\\). We can verify this with code as follows:",
